As your project mentor, here's an honest assessment with prioritized improvements:

🎯 Priority 1 — Reliability & Production Readiness
1. Rate Limit Retry Logic Right now when Gemini hits 429, it immediately switches to Bedrock. You should add a short retry with exponential backoff before switching — sometimes Gemini recovers in 2-3 seconds and Bedrock costs more.

2. Request Caching If two users analyze the exact same code, you're making duplicate AI calls. Add a simple MD5 hash of the code as a cache key in MongoDB. Saves cost + latency.

3. Request Timeout Handling If Gemini hangs (not a 429, just slow), your user waits forever. Add a 10-second timeout on AI calls so it always falls back gracefully.

🎯 Priority 2 — User Experience
4. Show Which AI Responded You already track _ai_provider in the response. Show it in the UI — a small badge like ⚡ Gemini or ☁️ Bedrock or 🔌 Offline builds user trust and is impressive to recruiters.

5. Analysis Loading Skeleton Replace the loading spinner with a skeleton screen that mimics the result panel layout. Feels much more premium.

6. Code Diff History In the sidebar history, let users compare two past analyses side by side. Very useful for learning.

🎯 Priority 3 — Code Quality & Architecture
7. Environment Validation on Startup Right now if GEMINI_API_KEY is missing, the app starts but silently fails. Add a startup check that logs a clear warning for each missing key.

8. Centralized Error Responses Your Flask routes have inconsistent error shapes. Create a single error_response(message, code) helper used everywhere.

9. Unit Tests for analyze_offline This function is now complex enough to need tests. Add a tests/ folder with 5-6 test cases (bubble sort → O(n²), binary search → O(log n), etc.). This is the #1 thing that impresses senior engineers reviewing your project.

🎯 Priority 4 — Portfolio & Career Value
10. Add a README Badge Board Add live badges to README.md:

[Python] [Flask] [React] [AWS Bedrock] [Gemini AI] [MongoDB] [Deployed on Render]
11. Record a Demo Video A 2-minute Loom showing the fallback chain in action (Gemini → Bedrock → Offline) is a massive portfolio differentiator.

12. Add CONTRIBUTING.md Makes the project look open-source ready and shows software engineering maturity.

Recommended Order to Tackle
Week 1  →  #4 (AI provider badge) + #7 (startup validation) + #9 (unit tests)
Week 2  →  #2 (caching) + #1 (retry logic) + #3 (timeout)
Week 3  →  #5 (skeleton loader) + #10 (README badges) + #11 (demo video)
Start with #9 (unit tests) — it's the highest-signal improvement for someone at your level and takes only a few hours. Want me to help with any of these?